# OOPs in Java (Object-Oriented Programming)

## 1) What is OOP?
Object-Oriented Programming is a way of writing programs by creating objects that contain both data and behavior.

Java is a fully object-oriented language because everything is based on classes and objects.

## 2) Main OOP Concepts in Java

### a) Class
A class is a blueprint or template for creating objects.

Example:
```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int rollNo;
}
```

### b) Object
An object is an instance of a class.

```java
Student s1 = new Student();
s1.name = "Amit";
s1.rollNo = 101;
```

### c) Encapsulation
Hiding internal data and exposing only necessary methods.

```java
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        balance += amount;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

### d) Inheritance
A class can inherit properties and methods from another class.

```java
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating...");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking...");
    }
}
```

### e) Polymorphism
One method can behave differently in different classes.

```java
class Shape {
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing shape");
    }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing circle");
    }
}
```

### f) Abstraction
Showing only essential features and hiding the implementation details.

```java
abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void start();
}
```

## 3) Constructor
A constructor is a special method used to initialize objects.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;

    Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

## 4) Method Overloading
Same method name but different parameters.

```java
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
```

## 5) Method Overriding
A child class provides a different implementation of a parent method.

```java
class Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent method");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Child method");
    }
}
```

## 6) Why OOP is useful
- Reusability
- Easy maintenance
- Better organization of code
- Scalable applications
- Real-world modeling

## 7) Simple OOP Example in Java
```java
class Person {
    String name;

    Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
    }
}

class Student extends Person {
    int rollNo;

    Student(String name, int rollNo) {
        super(name);
        this.rollNo = rollNo;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name + ", Roll No: " + rollNo);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student("Rakesh", 12);
        s.display();
    }
}
```

## 8) Summary
OOPs in Java helps us model real-world entities using classes and objects. The four main pillars are:
- Encapsulation
- Inheritance
- Polymorphism
- Abstraction

This is the foundation of Java programming.
